INT. MOUNTAIN VILLAGE DENTAL CLINIC - DAY

One chair. One lamp. A window onto a goat pasture. A bell tinkles.

DR. AMADEUS BRZEZINSKI-OYELARAN, 50s, white coat, mask, pulls on gloves. Beside him, ASSISTANT KALINDA ROSSITER-MWANGI, 30s, lays out instruments. In the chair: the village headman, ELDER ODYSSEUS PAPADAKIS-NGUYEN, 70s, jaw clenched. At the door, three villagers wait: TEODORA, 30s; JACOB, 20s; and a child, MIRIAM, 8, clutching a goat-shaped toy.

AMADEUS
Open, please.

ODYSSEUS
No.

AMADEUS
Elder, I need to look.

ODYSSEUS
Look from there.

KALINDA
(gently)
Sir. Just a peek.

He opens. Amadeus peers. Tuts.

AMADEUS
That is the worst molar I have seen in thirty years.

ODYSSEUS
(muffled)
Thank you.

AMADEUS
It's not a compliment.

ODYSSEUS
(muffled)
It is to me.

Calm. The clinic is bright and still. A goat bleats outside. Teodora and Jacob exchange glances. Miriam hugs her toy tighter.

AMADEUS
Kalinda. Local.

She hands him the syringe. He lifts it. The elder's eyes widen. The villagers lean forward.

Suddenly, a roar from the pasture. The door slams open and a goat charges in, horns down, bell clanging, scattering Teodora and Jacob and Miriam, who scream and leap onto the waiting chairs.

The goat skids on the tile, slams into the instrument tray. Steel clatters. The elder bolts upright. Amadeus drops the syringe. Kalinda lunges for the goat. It dodges. Everyone shouts at once.

ODYSSEUS
That's my goat!

AMADEUS
Your goat!

The goat, panting, plants itself in the dental chair, chewing a paper bib.
